osx-gpu: radeonsi on a big-endian host, part six: packed formats from their little-endian listing

On a big-endian host u_format_parse.py lists the channels of a bitmask
format (5_6_5, 4_4_4_4, 1_5_5_5, 2_10_10_10, 24_8: plain, not an array,
16 or 32 bits of unsigned or signed fields) from the top bits down and
remaps its swizzle to match; the shifts are numbered from the other end, so
each channel keeps its bits and Mesa's packed word means on a big-endian
host what it means on a little-endian one. radeonsi and ac_formats chose
the hardware data format, colour format, number type, component swap and
descriptor swizzle from that listing, channel by channel: B5G5R5A1 became
1_5_5_5 instead of 5_5_5_1, B5G6R5 got its red and blue swapped,
R10G10B10A2 had a 2-bit first channel and was no data format at all, and
Z24_UNORM_S8_UINT got the colour format 24_8 instead of 8_24 for the
depth-to-colour copies. That was the "two halves disagree" of part one,
whose answer was to withhold the formats. r600 had the same fault
(mesa/patches/0006).

ac_format_description() returns the little-endian listing for those
formats (built once from Mesa's table; identical to util_format_description
everywhere else and on a little-endian host), and every place that programs
the hardware from a format's channels or swizzle uses it: ac_formats.c
(colour format, number type, component swap, alpha position), the texture,
buffer and colour-buffer descriptors (ac_descriptors.c), and radeonsi's
sampler and vertex format checks, sampler-view swizzles, vertex elements
and DCC format compatibility. The CPU side needed nothing new: a packed
format is a host-order word in Mesa (texstore, u_format_table.c,
u_format_other.c, GL's packed client types) and the staging copy swaps it
by its whole word (si_be_swap_unit, part two). With both halves agreeing,
the formats are offered (RDN_BE_PACKED=0 withholds them as before) and
Mesa computes OpenGL 3.3 for Southern and Sea Islands instead of 2.1:
ARB_depth_buffer_float (Z32_FLOAT_S8X24_UINT), ARB_texture_rgb10_a2ui and
ARB_vertex_type_2_10_10_10_rev were the three it lacked, and 5_6_5,
4_4_4_4, 5_5_5_1, 10_10_10_2, 24_8 and 10F_11F_11F textures and render
targets are now the hardware's own instead of being converted to RGBA8 on
the CPU. tools/guest/begl33test checks each against the expected texels.

